\section {Introduction }
\label{sec:intro}

This technical note describes the VaLOR joint 3-flavour oscillation analysis. The joint analysis uses both the single $\mu$-like ring and single e-like ring SuperK 
samples, and makes a simultaneous fit of four oscillation parameters: $\sin^{2}\theta_{23}$, $|\Delta m_{32}^{2}|$, $\sin^{2}\theta_{13}$, and $\delta_{CP}$. In 
contrast to the standalone analyses, the joint fit does not assume prior values for any of these oscillation parameters. In this way, the interdependence 
between the oscillation parameters is taken into account when performing the joint fit of the 1 $\mu$-like ring and 1 e-like ring datasets.

This work builds on the 3-flavour $\nu_{\mu}$-disappearance analysis on the Run 1+2 dataset performed by the Rutherford-Oxford-Lancs group and reported in 
Ref.\cite{OurRun1and2Analysis3F}. In turn this was a continuation of the analyses performed by the same group in the 2-flavour $\nu_{\mu}$-disappearance framework 
and described in Refs.\ \cite{OurRun1and2Analysis2F, OurRun1DisappAnalysis2F}. A $\nu_{e}$-appearance analysis has also been developed in the same software 
framework in order to perform this joint analysis.

The joint oscillation analysis reported in this note was performed by the VaLOR (Valencia-Lancs-Oxford-Rutherford) group using NEUT \cite{Neut:ActaP}, and the 
results presented are from the combined Run1+2 dataset (2010b data) with an integrated exposure of 1.431$\times10^{20}$ POT. Oscillation probabilities were 
calculated in a full 3-flavour framework including matter effects. Further details of the 3-flavour oscillation analysis framework can be found in 
\cite{OurRun1and2Analysis3F}. The method for predicting the nominal SuperK reconstructed energy spectrum of single $\mu$-like ring events and the systematic 
uncertainties in that spectrum are the same as those described in Ref.\ \cite{OurRun1and2Analysis3F}. Additional systematic uncertainties relating to the 
$\nu_{e}$-appearance analysis were also included, and these were taken from Ref.\  \cite{NueRun1and2Analysis}.

The structure of this note is as follows. First, a motivation for the joint oscillation analysis is presented in Sec.\ \ref{sec:motivation}. The analysis method is 
described in detail in Sec.\ \ref{sec:description}. A study to validate the method using MC only is reported in Appendix \ref{app:fitterbias} of this note. The results 
from the combined Run 1+2 dataset are presented in Sec.\ \ref{sec:results}, and a summary is given in Sec.\ \ref{sec:summary}. Please note that there is also an 
appendix which reports an additional study of the $\theta_{23}$ center of symmetry. 




